netfilter: conntrack: make nf_conntrack_tcp_no_window_check per-netns

The MTK "optional tcp window check" patch keeps the toggle in a module
global, so every new network namespace re-registers the sysctl against
global data and register_net_sysctl_sz() emits

    sysctl net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_tcp_no_window_check:
    data points to kernel global data

plus a WARN() per namespace. daed, lxc, container runtimes and anything
else that unshares a netns trips it. A global also loses per-netns
semantics: writing the knob in one namespace leaks into every other one.

Follow the rework OpenWrt did for 5.15 (ansuelsmth, "generic: 5.15: fix
panic on tcp_no_window_check set with interface up"):

  - keep the value in struct nf_tcp_net as u8 next to tcp_be_liberal;
  - drop the hardcoded .data and bind the ctl_table entry through
    XASSIGN(NO_WINDOW_CHECK, &tn->tcp_no_window_check), with
    proc_dou8vec_minmax clamped to 0..1 like tcp_loose/tcp_be_liberal;
  - swap the two readers over to tn->tcp_no_window_check.

Divergence from that OpenWrt patch, deliberate: it seeds the field to 0 in
nf_conntrack_tcp_init_net(), which silently turns the bypass off and
leaves the knob doing nothing unless written by hand. The MTK feed keeps
this patch precisely because it wants the bypass on by default, and the
global it replaces was initialised to 1, so the field is seeded to 1 here.
Net behaviour is therefore identical to before, minus the warning.

The panic ansuelsmth fixed is also gone: the sysctl no longer points at
the init_net copy, so writing it while an interface is up updates the
owning namespace instead of racing a global.

--- a/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_proto_tcp.c
